You keep making this statement with the only evidence that serial ascenders
don't use it. In the past you've based this upon the classic protection
racket, where you must make it into minetown at XL1.

The protection racket is a virtually guaranteed play if you run it right.
I'll recap the list:

1) You don't have to be XL1. There is generally enough money in the upper 
level shops and in minetown to get all the protection you need even at XL3
or XL4.

2) You need to spend time buffing your pet and finding armor. Get your pet
to large. Try each piece of non cursed armor. Make sure you have a hard helm
before you get to the Mines.

3) Work on bumping up your max HP. Your god will bump you up to 5 points
if you are below 6 HP and less than 15 max HP @ XL1 or 20 max HP at XL2.
Generally with combinations of healing potion quaffing, god max HP bumping
and selective level rise you can get into the mid to upper 20s in HP at
XL2. For example I'm in minetown now with my current character at XL3 with
20HP and an armor class of -1 when I arrived. I'm at naked AC3 right now.

4) Learn to run away. Learn to lead monsters to your pet. Learn to close
doors. Learn to use traps effectively. Learn to use Elbereth for a breather.

I keep arguing it's not a low percentage play because I play it with every
character I play. I'm not a serial ascender not because of the MPR but
because of mistakes in mid and late game. For example in my last two games I
got my ass kicked by Master Kaen (101HP in a single round. Ouch!), and picked
up a cockatrice corpse without gloves (YASD!). But neither of those had
anything to do with the Minetown Protection Run (MPR).

The MPR attempts to balance the issues of AC, XL, and weight management as
you move into mid game. AC is a key survival tool. In mid to late game you'll
find enchant armor scrolls and holy water that'll give you all the AC you
need throughout the game. But in the early game you don't necessarily have
holy water or EA identified. You can improve your AC by wearing more stuff
but it weighs you down. Weight is an issue until you get that blessed bag of
holding for your stuff. But that doesn't help your armor. So less or lighter
armor is better. But again that's not necessarily available until mid game.
You can improve your survivability by increasing your XL but the dungeon
tends to level up with the character. 30th level wizards get dragons and
lichs on DL1 in the end game. XL1 characters do not.

So the MPR give ths character balance as they move into mid game. The extra
AC helps with survivability with each and every encounter. The protection AC
weighs nothing. The MPR isn't a death run when you do it right. With a XL9
pet, -7 AC, and 25 HP I can wander around the upper levels of the dungeon
virtually unscathed, even at XL1 or XL2. And after the run I can level up to
XL6 or XL7 in about 500 turns.

While I may agree that trying to make pacifist conduct ascension may be a low
percentage play, temporary restraining from leveling up in order to get
protection is not.


